Known-good-die economics matter more as packages enlarge
When multiple expensive dies are assembled onto one carrier, a failure in a single input can destroy the economic value of the completed package. Die screening, incoming inspection and traceability influence the true manufacturing cost. This is why production operators increasingly evaluate the full chain from wafer sort through final test rather than treating assembly as a single contracted service.

Substrate and cooling constraints can govern design choices
Dense routing on an interposer can provide bandwidth advantages, but power delivery and heat transfer must be engineered as the structure grows. Package substrate warpage changes alignment tolerance, while thermal gradients can weaken joints over repeated service cycles. Engineering teams should compare performance and reliability at the whole-module level before committing to a packaging architecture.

